<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # My Personal Goals for the Future Include... – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Future Plans and Goals</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> My Personal Goals for the Future Include...</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Concept: Explain abstract concepts, using simple analogies and native language when necessary. Concept: Aid in interpreting differences in communication.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can explain abstract career planning concepts, using simple analogies and visual aids (e.g., showing a road map image to illustrate "planning for a future"). Can aid peers in understanding differences in career communication, using both languages and visuals (e.g., showing a chart of different careers and their study paths). Can interpret classmates' future goals using gestures, visuals, and simple sentences to aid understanding across different communication abilities (e.g., pointing to a picture of a civil engineer and saying, "A civil engineer builds roads and bridges").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a series of images showing complex diagrams of different career paths (e.g., a flowchart for becoming a doctor vs. an entrepreneur). Students, in pairs, briefly discuss what they think each diagram means and try to explain it in 1-2 simple sentences, focusing on the core 'vision' or 'plan'. The teacher encourages using gestures and simple English to aid understanding. Example: pointing to a doctor's path and saying, "First, study hard. Then, become a doctor. Long plan."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to simplify an abstract concept by picking one diagram (e.g., "becoming an astronaut") and explaining it with simple analogies. "To be an astronaut, you *must* study science, like math (/θ/ sound). It's a big 'plan' for your 'future'. It's like building a very tall tower, step by step." The teacher emphasizes target grammar: "You *have to* train for many years. In 10 years, I *will be working* at NASA." The teacher also models the pronunciation of /θ/ in 'math' and /s' in 'mass' by contrasting them in simple sentences: "Math is important for science, but learning about the mass of objects is also useful."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'ambitious goal', does it mean an easy goal or a difficult goal that needs a lot of 'development'?" (Difficult, needs 'development')</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When we talk about 'future plans', are we talking about yesterday or tomorrow?" (Tomorrow/Later)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Future Plans and Goals".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short narrative excerpt (100-120 words) from a student's blog post titled "My 'Ambitious' Journey to a 'Business' 'Career'", describing their 'plan' for future 'development' in a complex field, including phrases like "I *have to* learn coding," "I *will be working* with a team," and "What are my next steps if I want to 'achieve' this 'vision'?" The excerpt includes some abstract terms related to business and technology. Register is informal, authentic for a blog.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Engage in collaborative goal-setting discussions for future academic or career planning, offering suggestions and feedback.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the blog post silently.
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main 'goal' or 'vision' of the student in the blog post?" (To have an 'ambitious' 'business' 'career' involving technology).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match abstract terms from the text (e.g., 'digital transformation', 'market strategy') with simple definitions provided on the board or worksheet. They *have to* interpret the meaning from context.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Peer Mediation Task:</b> In pairs, one student (A) explains the meaning of one of the abstract terms from the text to their partner (B), using an analogy or simple English, as if partner B did not understand it the first time. Partner B then asks a WH-question about the student's 'future' 'plan' based on the text (e.g., "What *will* the student *be doing* in five years?") and Partner A uses simple sentences to interpret the answer, including "I *plan to*..." or "I *want to*...".</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Explaining abstract concepts and interpreting communication differences. Grammar: Modals "have to", "must", WH-questions for future goals, Future continuous. Vocabulary: future, goals, plan, business, career, opportunity, vision, development, achieve, prepare, set, ambitious, important, future-oriented, if, when. Pronunciation: Future tense structures and modals, minimal pairs /θ/ and /s/ (e.g., 'math', 'mass'). Pragmatic: Discussing goals, responding to questions, offering encouragement.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Concept Explanation Match (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher provides a list of abstract career concepts (e.g., 'career development', 'ambitious vision', 'global opportunity') and another list of simple analogies or definitions. Students match each concept to its simplest explanation. Example: 'Career development' -> "getting better skills for your 'future' 'career'". This helps them *prepare* to explain these terms in their project. They *have to* match them carefully.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Simplification Chain (Semi-controlled Substitution Drill):</b> Students sit in groups of four. The teacher provides a complex sentence about a 'future' 'goal' (e.g., "Engaging in strategic cross-functional collaboration is crucial for project success if you *want to* 'achieve' your 'business' 'development' objectives."). The first student rephrases it using "have to" or "must" and simpler vocabulary. The next student simplifies it further, perhaps using an analogy or gestures. The third student asks a WH-question about the simplified 'plan', and the fourth student provides an 'ambitious' response using future continuous (e.g., "What *will* you *be doing* to 'achieve' this 'vision'? "In five years, I *will be working* to build strong teams."). This activity reinforces both grammar and the skill of simplifying for understanding. The teacher listens for correct pronunciation of modals and future forms.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Project Interpretation (Pair Interaction):</b> Students work in new pairs. Student A briefly describes an element of their "College/Career Fair Presentation" project (e.g., "My 'future' 'plan' is to be an engineer, I *must* study 'math' (/θ/ sound) and science."). Student B pretends not to fully understand and asks a clarifying WH-question (e.g., "What exactly *will* you *be doing* as an engineer?"). Student A then uses a visual aid (e.g., drawing, picture from phone), gestures, or a simple analogy to explain, potentially using a few words in their native language if necessary, according to the specific objective. (e.g., pointing to a picture of a civil engineer and saying, "A civil engineer builds roads and bridges."). Student B offers encouragement: "You *have to* work hard to 'achieve' your 'goals'!" The pairs switch roles.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Future Plans and Goals".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> "College/Career Fair Presentation - Mediation Role Play". Students present their prepared "College/Career Fair Presentation" project in front of a small group (3-4 students). Each presenter takes on the role of a "career advisor" or "project ambassador" whose main goal is to *mediate* information. The audience members act as "prospective students" with varying levels of understanding, asking clarifying WH-questions about the 'future' 'career', 'development', or 'opportunity'. The presenter *must* explain their 'plan' clearly, using simple analogies, visual aids (e.g., showing a road map image to illustrate "planning for a 'future'"), and being ready to simplify abstract concepts. They *have to* respond to questions like "What exactly *must* I do to 'prepare' for this 'career'?" or "What *will* I *be doing* in this field in the 'future'?" The presenter also offers encouragement and helps interpret different pathways or requirements, potentially using gestures or native language if a concept is particularly abstract, as stated in the specific objective.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> The activity directly uses the "College/Career Fair Presentation" project to simulate a real-world scenario where explaining 'future' 'plans' and 'goals' in an accessible way is crucial for others' understanding and 'development'. It emphasizes their 'vision' and the steps they *have to* take.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency, collaborative interaction, effective mediation of information, clarity of explanations, use of analogies, and completion of the communicative task by successfully helping peers understand complex 'future' 'goals' and career 'plans'. Correct use of modals of necessity ("have to", "must") and future continuous ("I will be working as...") is also expected.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on mediation skills and peer assessment for clarity and helpfulness.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform their "College/Career Fair Presentation - Mediation Role Play" for the teacher and peers. Peers use a simple checklist to evaluate the presenter's ability to explain, simplify, and aid understanding.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist & Peer Assessment):</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of explanation: Did the presenter simplify complex 'career' 'plans' and 'goals' effectively?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of analogies and visual aids: Were analogies, gestures, or visuals used to make abstract concepts ('vision', 'development') understandable? (e.g., showing a chart of different careers and their study paths).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of target grammar: Did the presenter use "have to," "must," WH-questions, and future continuous accurately when discussing 'future' 'plans'?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to interpret: Did the presenter respond to audience questions clearly and help them interpret information?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation: Was the pronunciation of 'future' tense structures and modals clear, including minimal pairs like /θ/ in 'math' and /s' in 'mass'?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and encouragement: Did the presenter engage the audience and offer 'encouragement'?</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of explaining your 'future' 'plan' or 'career' 'vision' simply?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did using visuals or analogies help you (or your peers) understand 'ambitious' 'goals' better?"</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Think about today's mediation activities. When would it be 'important' for you to explain 'ambitious' 'future' 'goals' or complex 'career' information in a simple way in real life?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you 'must' do or 'have to' 'plan' for to improve your ability to help others understand your 'vision' for the 'future'?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul style="margin-top: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students mediate abstract concepts and interpret information about 'future' 'plans' and 'goals'? (e.g., specific analogies, visual aids, peer interaction format).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled to simplify ideas or use the target grammar for explanation? (e.g., more structured sentence frames for explanations, explicit mini-lessons on analogy creation). How can I better address pronunciation of /θ/ and /s/ in context?</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This is Lesson 5, the final lesson in this theme sequence. The next lesson will transition to a new theme, applying the integrated language skills (listening, reading, speaking, writing, and mediation) to new content, building on the students' improved ability to articulate and interpret their 'future' 'plans' and 'vision'.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>